## 2. Select the OKD Version

Before downloading the OKD installer, CLI, or operating system image, first decide which OKD release will be installed.

For this lab, the selected release is:

```text
OKD 4.22
Architecture: x86_64
```

The version should be selected first because the installation tools, release artifacts, and operating system image need to correspond to the cluster release.

### 2.1 Check the Available OKD Releases

OKD releases are published through the OKD project release repository.

Open the official OKD releases page:

https://github.com/okd-project/okd/releases

Look for the required stable release.

For this lab, OKD 4.22 was selected.

### 2.2 Why OKD 4.22 Was Selected

The version should not be chosen only because it is the newest release.

Before selecting a release, consider:

- Is it a stable release?
- Is the required architecture available?
- Are the required installation artifacts available?
- Is the release supported by the installation documentation?
- Are the required operating system images available?
- Are the installer and `oc` client available for the selected release?

For this lab:

```text
OKD Release : 4.22
Architecture: x86_64
Platform    : OCI
Installation: UPI
```

The OKD 4.22 documentation provides installation procedures for user-provisioned infrastructure and platform-agnostic/bare-metal style installations. :contentReference[oaicite:1]{index=1}

### 2.3 Check the Machine Architecture

The OCI compute instances used in this lab are x86_64/AMD64 based.

Verify the architecture of the installation host:

```bash
uname -m
```

Expected output:

```text
x86_64
```

You can also verify with:

```bash
arch
```

Expected output:

```text
x86_64
```

### 2.4 Verify the Selected Architecture

The architecture used by the installation tools and operating system image must match the architecture of the machines.

For this lab:

```text
Installation host → x86_64
OCI Compute        → x86_64
OKD artifacts      → x86_64
OS image           → x86_64
```

Do not download an ARM64/aarch64 image for an x86_64 OCI instance.

### 2.5 Record the Version

Set the selected version as a shell variable.

For this lab:

```bash
export OKD_VERSION=4.22
```

Verify:

```bash
echo "$OKD_VERSION"
```

Expected output:

```text
4.22
```

This variable will be used in the following sections when constructing download paths and identifying release artifacts.

### 2.6 Record the Release Architecture

Set the architecture:

```bash
export RELEASE_ARCH=x86_64
```

Verify:

```bash
echo "$RELEASE_ARCH"
```

Expected output:

```text
x86_64
```

### 2.7 Verify the Selected Release Information

Display both variables together:

```bash
printf 'OKD Version : %s\nArchitecture: %s\n' "$OKD_VERSION" "$RELEASE_ARCH"
```

Expected output:

```text
OKD Version : 4.22
Architecture: x86_64
```

At this point, the installation environment has a defined target:

```text
OKD 4.22
    │
    └── x86_64
```

### 2.8 Version Selection Checklist

Before downloading the installation tools, verify:

```text
[ ] OKD release has been selected
[ ] Selected release is stable
[ ] Installation method is UPI
[ ] Target platform is OCI
[ ] Installation architecture is x86_64
[ ] OCI compute architecture is x86_64
[ ] OKD_VERSION variable is set
[ ] RELEASE_ARCH variable is set
```

For this lab:

```text
OKD_VERSION=4.22
RELEASE_ARCH=x86_64
```

